  "verdict": "All 8 probability functions reproduce hftbacktest to 1.1e-16 on identical inputs \u2014 the port is exact. Across 5 generative regimes swept rather than chosen, a probability function estimates the queue better than the best constant in 3 of 5 in sample and 3 of 5 held out, every margin with its whole 95% interval above zero. It loses in 2: front-sticky \u00b7 the front never cancels; adversarial \u00b7 front-sticky and replenishing \u2014 where the front of the queue never cancels, 'every cancellation is behind you' is not a naive assumption but the exact truth, and no member of the family can express it. That regime is the model's documented failure case, and reporting it is the difference between a sweep and a demonstration. One result cuts against the model and is reported rather than dropped: in \u03b3=0.5 \u00b7 cancels spread toward the back it estimates the queue better and still costs more (-0.020bps), because a better *average* position estimate can sit on the optimistic side at exactly the moments that decide a fill. Queue accuracy and execution cost are not the same objective, and a capability that only ever reported the metric it wins on would never have found that."
}